<h2 id="apps-heading">Transfer Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Packaging line routing:</strong> <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">Packaging lines</a> use transfers to route products between parallel process lines. Cases divert from a main transport line to one of several case packers, labelers, or palletizers using pop-up chain transfers. The PLC routes each case based on product SKU, destination, or load-balancing logic.</p>
<p><strong>Pallet handling:</strong> <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">Pallet conveyor systems</a> use right-angle chain transfers and turntables to route loaded pallets between stretch wrappers, labelers, and shipping lanes. The heavy loads (2,000-4,000 lbs) require positive chain drive or turntable rotation. <a href="/conveyor-types/cdlr-conveyors/">CDLR conveyors</a> feed right-angle chain transfers for heavy pallet routing.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution center sortation:</strong> Transfer conveyors divert totes and cartons from trunk lines to pick zones, pack stations, and shipping lanes. High-volume operations use high-speed shoe sorters or pop-up wheel diverters (300-600 FPM). Lower-volume operations use pop-up chain transfers or push diverters.</p>
<p><strong>Reject and quality control:</strong> <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">Reject systems</a> use push diverters to remove failed products from the main conveyor line. Checkweighers, metal detectors, and vision systems trigger the diverter to push rejects onto a spur conveyor or into a reject bin. Push diverters offer the fastest cycle time (1-2 seconds) for reject applications.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">Collating and laning:</a></strong> Transfer conveyors distribute products from a single infeed lane across multiple output lanes. Each transfer diverts a programmed number of products to fill lanes evenly before <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loading</a> or case packing.</p>
<p><strong>Vertical integration:</strong> Transfer conveyors connect horizontal conveyor runs to <a href="/conveyor-types/vertical-reciprocating/">vertical reciprocating conveyors (VRCs)</a> and spiral conveyors. A right-angle transfer feeds product onto the VRC platform or spiral infeed, and another transfer receives product at the upper level.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Transfer Conveyor</h2>
<p><strong>Product weight drives the first cut.</strong> Products under 100 lbs qualify for pop-up chains, pop-up balls, or push diverters. Products from 100-500 lbs need pop-up chain or right-angle chain transfers. Products over 500 lbs require right-angle chain transfers or turntables. <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">Pallet loads</a> over 2,000 lbs typically use turntables or heavy-duty right-angle chain transfers.</p>
<p><strong>Throughput determines cycle time requirements.</strong> Calculate the maximum number of transfers per minute needed. Divide 60 seconds by the required transfers per minute to get the maximum allowable cycle time. Pop-up chains at 2-4 seconds deliver 15-30 transfers per minute. Turntables at 6-10 seconds deliver 6-10 transfers per minute. If throughput exceeds single-transfer capacity, add parallel transfers or switch to high-speed sortation technology.</p>
<p><strong>Orientation sensitivity matters.</strong> Push diverters can rotate products during the push. Pop-up chain transfers and right-angle transfers maintain product orientation. Turntables intentionally rotate the product. If barcode labels, product graphics, or downstream equipment require a specific product orientation, avoid push diverters.</p>
<p><strong>Direction flexibility.</strong> Pop-up chains and right-angle transfers handle 90-degree transfers only. Pop-up ball transfers and turntables handle any angle. Push diverters typically push at 90 degrees but angled sweeps can push at 30-60 degrees.</p>
<p>Transfers must integrate with upstream <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation zones</a> that meter products into the transfer at the correct spacing and timing. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyor zones</a> upstream of the transfer provide precise product positioning for reliable transfer operation.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a transfer conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A transfer conveyor changes product direction between two conveyor lines, typically at 90 degrees. Types include pop-up chain, pop-up ball, right-angle chain, turntable, and push diverter. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/">complete conveyor type guide</a> for the full picture.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the difference between a pop-up transfer and a push diverter?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Pop-up transfers lift chains or balls above the roller surface to carry the product sideways. Push diverters physically push the product off the line. Pop-ups maintain orientation and handle higher loads. Push diverters are cheaper and faster but may rotate the product.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How fast can a transfer conveyor operate?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Pop-up chain: 30-120 FPM, 2-4 sec cycle. Push diverter: up to 200 FPM, 1-2 sec cycle. Turntable: 6-10 sec cycle. High-speed sortation diverters handle 300-600 FPM. Choose based on your transfers-per-minute requirement.</p>

</div>
